WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.148 --> 00:00:01.638
- You can swim in my tears,

00:00:01.638 --> 00:00:04.725
that's how much I cried in law school.

00:00:04.725 --> 00:00:07.308
(quirky music)

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:13.021
- So I went into law school
doe-eyed, ready to work hard,

00:00:13.021 --> 00:00:16.269
but not really understanding
what I was getting into.

00:00:16.269 --> 00:00:17.947
- I feel like I blocked a lot of it out.

00:00:17.947 --> 00:00:20.233
Law school is traumatizing.

00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:22.611
- Basically, you're
with the same 80 people

00:00:22.611 --> 00:00:26.148
for all of your classes the
entire first year of law school.

00:00:26.148 --> 00:00:27.808
You would end class and stand outside

00:00:27.808 --> 00:00:31.105
and just like talk to each
other about law school.

00:00:31.105 --> 00:00:33.713
- Little groups, you
huddle in little groups

00:00:33.713 --> 00:00:35.307
about how much you hate law school,

00:00:35.307 --> 00:00:37.582
and then shuffle over
to the library together.

00:00:37.582 --> 00:00:38.850
- Yeah, together.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.450
Like, alright, I guess I'll study now.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:42.534
- Like little flamingos.

00:00:42.534 --> 00:00:45.462
- So cold-calling is
basically, you're in class

00:00:45.462 --> 00:00:46.969
and then the professor calls on people.

00:00:46.969 --> 00:00:51.666
So he might say Tina, "What
was the courts rationale?"

00:00:51.666 --> 00:00:54.665
and like, "Well what did
the dissenting opinion say?"

00:00:54.665 --> 00:00:57.196
And not only question, so
you know if your professor

00:00:57.196 --> 00:00:59.571
called on you, you're
going to be cold-called

00:00:59.571 --> 00:01:00.692
for the entire case.

00:01:00.692 --> 00:01:02.453
So you better know it inside and out.

00:01:02.453 --> 00:01:04.555
Make inferences about it.

00:01:04.555 --> 00:01:07.854
Like have an informed,
critical opinion of the case.

00:01:07.854 --> 00:01:10.211
And he might leave you for
a little bit and come back

00:01:10.211 --> 00:01:13.596
and be like, "Tina, what
happen in Paul's graph?"

00:01:13.596 --> 00:01:14.992
- Right, and you think you're safe,

00:01:14.992 --> 00:01:16.754
for that moment that he leaves.

00:01:16.754 --> 00:01:19.196
You're like, I got this, I slay, queen.

00:01:19.196 --> 00:01:21.060
You walk away thinking your Beyonce

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.437
and then come back and
get hit in the face again

00:01:23.437 --> 00:01:25.836
with questions that you can't answer

00:01:25.836 --> 00:01:28.539
cause you have no idea what's going on.

00:01:28.539 --> 00:01:29.909
- So this class, he did
a little differently.

00:01:29.909 --> 00:01:32.971
He would right a letter on the board.

00:01:32.971 --> 00:01:34.098
And so you would walk
in, look at the board,

00:01:34.098 --> 00:01:36.828
and it's like, "Oh yes, the letter is "S",

00:01:36.828 --> 00:01:39.043
my last name starts with a "C"".

00:01:39.043 --> 00:01:40.174
I'm safe for today.

00:01:40.174 --> 00:01:41.764
You knew right at the
get-go if you weren't

00:01:41.764 --> 00:01:43.433
going to be on-call so
you could kind of relax.

00:01:43.433 --> 00:01:47.028
But if your letter was on the
board you're just like scared.

00:01:47.028 --> 00:01:49.525
I had never missed a class,
people don't miss class

00:01:49.525 --> 00:01:51.165
in law school also, by the way.

00:01:51.165 --> 00:01:52.986
Anyway, so I missed this class

00:01:52.986 --> 00:01:54.536
because I had a doctors appointment.

00:01:54.536 --> 00:01:56.939
That was the day my
letter was on the board

00:01:56.939 --> 00:02:00.672
and I had text messages
from like 10 people in class

00:02:00.672 --> 00:02:02.187
asking me where I was.

00:02:02.187 --> 00:02:05.787
So I guess my professor called
my name like three times

00:02:05.787 --> 00:02:10.086
and I wasn't there and it
was actually so humiliating.

00:02:10.086 --> 00:02:13.030
The professor I don't think
even remembers that probably.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:17.863
But every single day feel so
ashamed, embarrassed, scared.

00:02:19.377 --> 00:02:21.544
Like nothings going to
happen if you get cold-called

00:02:21.544 --> 00:02:23.059
and don't know the answer.

00:02:23.059 --> 00:02:24.103
- It is definitely public shaming.

00:02:24.103 --> 00:02:24.986
- Yeah.

00:02:24.986 --> 00:02:28.937
- On my birthday, we took
our first criminal law exam.

00:02:28.937 --> 00:02:32.373
I slept through my class the
next day, through that morning

00:02:32.373 --> 00:02:33.472
and I missed class.

00:02:33.472 --> 00:02:35.639
So twelve o'clock rolls around, I wake up,

00:02:35.639 --> 00:02:38.472
I don't really know how sober I am

00:02:40.259 --> 00:02:42.331
but you can't miss class.

00:02:42.331 --> 00:02:45.861
So I throw on my girly
pink running shorts,

00:02:45.861 --> 00:02:48.362
I run from the mission district through it

00:02:48.362 --> 00:02:51.319
to the tenderloin, which is
like a 10, 15 minute run.

00:02:51.319 --> 00:02:55.535
So I'm covered in sweat, I
have Pedialyte as my breakfast,

00:02:55.535 --> 00:03:00.397
and I have my aviator sunglasses
and I stroll into class

00:03:00.397 --> 00:03:02.508
and I'm like, yes I got this.

00:03:02.508 --> 00:03:04.320
I don't got this.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:07.575
So I'm in class, everybody
is already staring at me

00:03:07.575 --> 00:03:09.612
because I'm suppose to be on-call.

00:03:09.612 --> 00:03:12.319
It's civil procedure, I have
no idea what's going on.

00:03:12.319 --> 00:03:14.462
The professor is sitting
in front of our lecture.

00:03:14.462 --> 00:03:17.064
He turns to me and we make eye contact

00:03:17.064 --> 00:03:18.701
and for a moment in my life I'm like,

00:03:18.701 --> 00:03:20.684
this is it, this is when I go, Mom.

00:03:20.684 --> 00:03:23.443
I'm sorry, I love you, I've shamed you.

00:03:23.443 --> 00:03:26.039
So I felt myself about to throw up

00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:29.606
and we're locking eyes and I
can't feel it like a chipmunk.

00:03:29.606 --> 00:03:34.445
Just culminating in my mouth
because I'm so nervous.

00:03:34.445 --> 00:03:36.293
By this time I'm a marathon runner,

00:03:36.293 --> 00:03:38.357
I just ran to the tenderloin,
I just ran to school.

00:03:38.357 --> 00:03:43.028
So I run into the bathroom
and he didn't even call on me.

00:03:43.028 --> 00:03:46.447
So I puke in the bathroom for no reason

00:03:46.447 --> 00:03:50.263
and I'm too ashamed to
go back into the class.

00:03:50.263 --> 00:03:52.365
So my ride or die law school homie,

00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:56.513
she luckily picks up my stuff
and just kind of walks it out

00:03:56.513 --> 00:03:59.268
and everyone just forgets
that ever happened.

00:03:59.268 --> 00:04:03.030
It was traumatizing though,
ruined that bathroom.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:06.113
So I was sent to Texas for Moot Court

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:09.061
and it was the Houston competition.

00:04:09.061 --> 00:04:10.709
- Moot court is like mock trial,

00:04:10.709 --> 00:04:12.332
but it is a little different.

00:04:12.332 --> 00:04:15.893
- It's less exciting
and theatrical, I think.

00:04:15.893 --> 00:04:17.545
- We don't get to yell out, "I object,

00:04:17.545 --> 00:04:18.769
- Yeah, no.

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:20.339
- "You can't handle the truth."

00:04:20.339 --> 00:04:22.422
One really old lawyer man

00:04:23.925 --> 00:04:27.036
comes up to us and he
was just talking to us,

00:04:27.036 --> 00:04:27.994
it was really early in the morning

00:04:27.994 --> 00:04:29.338
and he looks at me and goes,

00:04:29.338 --> 00:04:32.332
"I'm surprised they let women argue."

00:04:32.332 --> 00:04:33.856
Silence.

00:04:33.856 --> 00:04:35.106
From our table.

00:04:36.209 --> 00:04:40.395
And we're from San Francisco
area so we're not use

00:04:40.395 --> 00:04:42.501
to the type of discrimination or at least,

00:04:42.501 --> 00:04:44.133
I'm not use to the type of discrimination

00:04:44.133 --> 00:04:47.024
that I would have to face in Texas.

00:04:47.024 --> 00:04:49.131
And without missing a
beat, I just looked at him

00:04:49.131 --> 00:04:51.862
and was like, "I look forward
to surprising every body."

00:04:51.862 --> 00:04:53.633
And it was something
that I necessarily wasn't

00:04:53.633 --> 00:04:56.633
prepared for coming into law school.

00:04:57.535 --> 00:05:00.780
I didn't realize how
much biased and prejudice

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.841
I would get from current attorneys,

00:05:03.841 --> 00:05:07.064
especially being a woman of
color, first generation law.

00:05:07.064 --> 00:05:08.370
But it was something in Texas that kind

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.048
of hit me in the face.

00:05:10.048 --> 00:05:12.238
And that was my terrible experience.

00:05:12.238 --> 00:05:14.268
It just threw me for the whole competition

00:05:14.268 --> 00:05:18.873
so I did end up rolling my
eyes and saying "Mhm" a lot.

00:05:18.873 --> 00:05:21.240
I got a little feisty, I got
a little attitude over there.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:22.711
- Yeah, I would leave, I feel like.

00:05:22.711 --> 00:05:23.727
- We didn't get very far.

00:05:23.727 --> 00:05:25.532
- Yeah that's for the best probably.

00:05:25.532 --> 00:05:26.365
- Yeah.

00:05:26.365 --> 00:05:27.198
(laughing)

00:05:27.198 --> 00:05:28.866
- I'm banned from Texas.

00:05:28.866 --> 00:05:30.118
- Yeah, goodbye.

00:05:30.118 --> 00:05:33.275
I had an exam, it was my
first year of law school,

00:05:33.275 --> 00:05:36.600
my first semester, so it
was one of my first exams.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:39.017
I studied a lot, I felt fine.

00:05:40.092 --> 00:05:41.654
Just as I'm ready to get into the exam

00:05:41.654 --> 00:05:44.735
I go to the library to just
review things very quickly

00:05:44.735 --> 00:05:46.157
before going into the classroom.

00:05:46.157 --> 00:05:50.468
And I look over to my left
and there's a girl in my class

00:05:50.468 --> 00:05:54.463
a smart girl, like someone
who should be feeling

00:05:54.463 --> 00:05:58.401
better than me, is
sobbing in a study room.

00:05:58.401 --> 00:06:00.737
Like crying, sobbing.

00:06:00.737 --> 00:06:02.314
That just really tore up my life.

00:06:02.314 --> 00:06:03.928
- Confession session.

00:06:03.928 --> 00:06:05.754
It wasn't me.

00:06:05.754 --> 00:06:07.995
I walked into my civil procedure class,

00:06:07.995 --> 00:06:09.834
which is funny because I'm attorney now

00:06:09.834 --> 00:06:12.120
and I do nothing but civil procedure.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:14.516
But in law school it's terrifying.

00:06:14.516 --> 00:06:16.347
I walk in, I sit down.

00:06:16.347 --> 00:06:18.640
My little sister was hyping me up all day.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:20.697
She was just like, you got this, you slay,

00:06:20.697 --> 00:06:22.356
walk in there like you own it.

00:06:22.356 --> 00:06:26.477
I do and then I open the
first page of my booklet,

00:06:26.477 --> 00:06:28.630
I look down and I just start balling.

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:30.382
In the exam room, just crying.

00:06:30.382 --> 00:06:31.861
And I try to look cute because I figure

00:06:31.861 --> 00:06:34.308
if I'm going to fail, I'm
going to look fly as I fail.

00:06:34.308 --> 00:06:37.061
And I did it, mascara
was running everywhere.

00:06:37.061 --> 00:06:40.483
My cat eye was not cute like
it is, it was everywhere.

00:06:40.483 --> 00:06:41.895
Half of my eye brow wasn't even done,

00:06:41.895 --> 00:06:43.391
I don't even know how that happened.

00:06:43.391 --> 00:06:45.547
It was the trauma of civil procedure

00:06:45.547 --> 00:06:48.101
and so I had to work through tears.

00:06:48.101 --> 00:06:49.273
And my laptop, I was like

00:06:49.273 --> 00:06:50.945
great, now there's water everywhere,

00:06:50.945 --> 00:06:52.708
it's gonna explode then I'm going

00:06:52.708 --> 00:06:54.911
to burn the school down accidentally.

00:06:54.911 --> 00:06:57.021
So that is my confession
about how I cried.

00:06:57.021 --> 00:06:57.892
- So you did cry?

00:06:57.892 --> 00:06:58.725
- I cried.

00:06:58.725 --> 00:07:02.170
- It really does break you
down, like really tests you.

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:03.912
- I don't cry now, anymore.

00:07:03.912 --> 00:07:05.592
Except when I'm watching This is Us.

00:07:05.592 --> 00:07:09.675
- You'll cry a lot, you
won't recognize yourself.

